1
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,600
The working day
begins for one of the men

2
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:25,200
who in Spain are as
legendary as they are infamous.

3
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:47,280
Debt collectors in tails have
been around for 30 years.

4
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:52,760
The company’s heyday was
during the 2008 financial crisis,

5
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:55,480
when thousands of
Spaniards went bankrupt.

6
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:58,240
In the worst cases, debtors
are confronted on the street.

7
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:10,160
Today, the whole world
is in the same position

8
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:11,960
as the Spaniard who can’t pay.

9
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:15,160
Private individuals,
companies and whole countries

10
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:18,760
have been struggling with steadily
rising debts for over 15 years.

11
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:21,640
The problem has been
severely aggravated

12
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:24,560
by the Corona pandemic
and the war in Ukraine.

13
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:27,240
Does this mean
the world is about to

14
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:29,760
collapse under a
huge mountain of debt?

15
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:36,000
The world’s total debt currently
exceeds 300 trillion dollars.

16
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:40,520
That’s a colossal number
with 14 zeros.

17
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:43,000
To pay off this debt,
everyone in the world

18
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,320
would have to work three
and a half years without pay.

19
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:02,760
To find out what’s
going on in the world,

20
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:06,000
we decide to explore the
stories behind global debt.

21
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:12,480
We want to find out what’s
happening away from the headlines.

22
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,760
We visit people in
debt and learn about

23
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:20,240
the dramatic impact
it has on their lives.

24
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:26,200
And we go to countries with
vast levels of national debt.

25
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:31,960
We want to find out how the lives
of ordinary people are affected.

26
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,280
Is the situation hopeless?

27
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:39,320
Or are there any ways
out of the debt trap?

28
00:03:55,520 --> 00:04:00,320
After the financial crisis of 2008,
many states fell into debt.

29
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:08,200
Back then, after a phase of
unbridled lending in the USA,

30
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:09,880
a property bubble burst.

31
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:14,200
Major banks went bust and
the stock market collapsed.

32
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:20,760
The US government had to take on
staggering debts to save the economy.

33
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:23,320
The debt crisis in the USA

34
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:26,800
triggered a global chain
reaction that still resonates.

35
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:33,600
To trace the long-term
impact of the 2008 crisis,

36
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:36,680
we travelled to a country where
money seems to be no object.

37
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:40,880
The Emirate of Dubai.

38
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:54,600
There we meet Sajjad Maroof,
an estate agent,

39
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,000
who wants to show us
something exquisite.

40
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:02,080
A map of the world
consisting of artificial islands.

41
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:07,840
In 2003, a state-owned corporation
began constructing The World Islands

42
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:09,800
and selling them to investors.

43
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:13,760
They wanted to build
retreats for the super-rich,

44
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:15,560
palaces like this one.

45
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:30,240
Welcome to Sweden Beach Palace,
welcome to the home of Vikings.

46
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,800
Only the kings are
allowed to stay over here.

47
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:38,320
The palace provides
an astonishing refuge

48
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:42,320
from the outdoor temperatures
topping 45 degrees Celsius.

49
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:45,240
Scandinavian people
they have a tradition

50
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:48,320
to take sauna to open
up the body pores.

51
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:50,360
So we have a sauna over here.

52
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:53,320
We can make it as
a dry or a steam room.

53
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:58,280
Then immediately, when sauna is
done they have to take an ice bath.

54
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:00,840
So we have our own snow room.

55
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:07,440
That's how you enjoy the snow.

56
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:23,160
So the idea was this: How to
bring the whole world to Dubai.

57
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:28,200
So that’s Spain, you can see Portugal
over there, and that’s France.

58
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:35,280
We try to follow
Mr Maroof’s logic,

59
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:40,160
but we find it difficult to make out
the outlines of European countries.

60
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:44,560
The sharp contours
have eroded away.

61
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:49,760
Since 2008, the project called
“The World” has lain dormant.

62
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:56,080
Nevertheless, one man still wants to

63
00:06:56,080 --> 00:07:00,200
keep building on the artificial
islands and create a mini Europe.

64
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:03,840
Josef Kleindienst,
an Austrian businessman,

65
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:06,080
has wound up here
in the Persian Gulf.

66
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:09,680
Part of his grand vision
can already be admired.

67
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:14,480
Welcome to see our
Heart of Europe project.

68
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:19,360
I want to point out first the
location, where we are right now.

69
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:22,560
We are here in the roof of
Sweden Beach Palace number one,

70
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:27,440
overlooking the Germany island,
32 villas on the beach and pool.

71
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:33,680
And you can see the “Strandkörbe”
already waiting for tourists.

72
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:40,920
You see here our main island,
Europe, hosting our hotels.

73
00:07:41,320 --> 00:07:44,400
Monaco Hotel was
our first hotel to open,

74
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:47,440
and it is a party hotel: 24/7 party.

75
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,680
This is typical of Dubai,
the epitome of abundance.

76
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:58,800
A Switzerland
beneath palm trees

77
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:02,000
welcoming anyone who wants
to take their wealth to the emirate.

78
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:08,200
Postmodern towers
soar up into the sky.

79
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:11,280
They include the tallest
building in the world.

80
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,040
The Burj Khalifa skyscraper

81
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,920
was originally going
to be called Burj Dubai.

82
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,040
But the prestigious building
had to be renamed

83
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:25,920
after the ruler of Abu Dhabi,
Sheikh Khalifa.

84
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:32,760
The powerful neighbor helped Dubai
out with an emergency loan in 2008.

85
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:38,520
The emirate with relatively little
oil was on the verge of bankruptcy.

86
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:43,400
Half-finished buildings bear witness

87
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:47,160
to the credit-leveraged
madness a few years ago.

88
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:53,760
Instead of lavish real estate,

89
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:56,880
it would have been better to
invest in more sensible things,

90
00:08:56,880 --> 00:09:00,120
says sovereign debt
expert Christoph Trebesch.

91
00:09:36,360 --> 00:09:39,920
When the inevitable crash
hit Dubai’s real estate market,

92
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:42,640
many international
investors pulled out.

93
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:49,400
They included some who’d invested in
the painstakingly created world map.

94
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:57,080
Some went bankrupt,
others were left

95
00:09:57,080 --> 00:09:59,760
with piles of sand
whose value plummeted.

96
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:06,440
One even committed suicide
during the debt crisis.

97
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:11,720
The other Europeans,
they did not have the trust to stay,

98
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:14,240
or maybe they ran out of
money or out of funding,

99
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:16,480
and it was not easy in 2008.

100
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:20,360
And then we realized
there are chances.

101
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:22,280
When 40 others run away,

102
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:25,560
you have a chance to take over
part of their business, for example.

103
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:37,400
Even though “The World” keeps
stalling, life is creeping into it.

104
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:40,640
Recently, another investor
began building

105
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:43,640
on the island that
represents Argentina.

106
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:54,400
Despite its past as a debtor,
Dubai’s image hasn’t been tarnished.

107
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:58,200
The emirate was lucky
to get help so quickly.

108
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:10,280
At the southernmost
end of the world

109
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:13,760
lies the undisputed bankruptcy
champion of our planet.

110
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:18,400
Argentina has been
bankrupt nine times.

111
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:22,680
Sometimes international
financiers stepped in;

112
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:25,440
sometimes no aid was received.

113
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:30,320
We can see here what happens
when a state has too much debt

114
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:33,200
to meet its obligations
to the poorest people.

115
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:38,160
The slum called Villa 31

116
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:42,160
spreads beneath the motorway
bridges in central Buenos Aires.

117
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:51,600
Thousands of Argentines
rely on charities.

118
00:11:54,360 --> 00:11:55,600
A century ago,

119
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:59,200
Argentina was one of the five
richest countries in the world.

120
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:04,200
It has mineral resources and
a well-educated population.

121
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:08,600
How could such a country
fall so low?

122
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:19,800
Alejandro Bercovich is an
economist and radio journalist.

123
00:12:20,320 --> 00:12:24,800
He’s been studying why
Argentina can’t escape the debt trap.

124
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:19,760
The result is ever
higher deficits.

125
00:13:19,960 --> 00:13:24,440
And being so badly in debt, Argentina
can’t always borrow money abroad.

126
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:28,160
But it still has to pay pensions
and unemployment benefits,

127
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:29,840
so it uses a trick.

128
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:37,720
Argentina simply prints large
amounts of its own currency, the peso.

129
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:45,560
As we can see here, there’s a money
supply that can be used to buy goods.

130
00:13:46,480 --> 00:13:50,600
In order to meet the costs of the
state, more and more money is printed.

131
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:53,400
But the quantity of
goods remains the same.

132
00:13:54,520 --> 00:13:56,800
People can buy more
with the new money.

133
00:13:59,520 --> 00:14:03,240
Retailers respond to higher
demand by raising their prices.

134
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:07,080
Inflation takes hold.

135
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:20,920
Many Argentines can’t
afford everyday items.

136
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:26,080
So people meet up to barter.

137
00:14:29,080 --> 00:14:31,880
Meetings are arranged on
Facebook and WhatsApp

138
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:33,800
and then the group meets here.

139
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:40,840
Instead of money, they use points
to determine the value of each item.

140
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:43,680
Clothes, for example,
can then be swapped

141
00:14:43,680 --> 00:14:46,360
for personal hygiene items and food.

142
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:53,920
Bartering looks set to
persist for a long time.

143
00:14:57,040 --> 00:15:00,960
In Argentina, there’s no
end in sight to the price spiral.

144
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:18,560
Inflation is now a serious problem
in the northern hemisphere, too.

145
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:22,320
In wealthy Germany,
more and more people are suffering

146
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:24,720
from price rises
for energy and food.

147
00:15:26,440 --> 00:15:29,400
The share of income that low
earners spend on living costs

148
00:15:29,400 --> 00:15:31,120
is disproportionately high.

149
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:35,360
That’s why the poorest people
fall into debt especially often.

150
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:44,760
Duisburg is a city with many
over-indebted people in Germany.

151
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:48,600
16% of its inhabitants
are on the debt register,

152
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:51,920
compared to not even
half that many in Munich.

153
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:56,400
Frank Wendler,
a local jack-of-all-trades,

154
00:15:56,400 --> 00:16:00,240
knows first-hand just how bad
it is to be hopelessly in debt.

155
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:48,600
For decades,
Wendler sold gas cannisters.

156
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:51,560
But the business had its pitfalls.

157
00:17:13,480 --> 00:17:15,240
Fate took its course.

158
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:19,480
After a tax audit, he owed
a large amount of back tax.

159
00:17:56,920 --> 00:18:00,960
Like its citizens, Duisburg is
also struggling with a debt trap.

160
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:05,680
Starting with the crisis in the
steel industry in the mid-1980s,

161
00:18:05,680 --> 00:18:07,280
things went downhill.

162
00:18:07,800 --> 00:18:11,360
And the once affluent city now
has an army of unemployed.

163
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:21,280
Germany has been
systematically saving for decades.

164
00:18:21,920 --> 00:18:23,360
The debt level is low...

165
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:31,320
but the result is potholes, bridges in
need of repair, and rundown railways.

166
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:41,440
With Duisburg’s drastic
cuts now behind it,

167
00:18:41,560 --> 00:18:44,080
a Consolidation Pact
has been introduced

168
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:46,800
to make the city more
attractive for investors.

169
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:50,040
Nearly half of its debt
has been paid off,

170
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:54,920
partly thanks to the development
of Duisburg as a logistics hub.

171
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:07,800
The Port of Duisburg is the
biggest inland port in the world.

172
00:19:09,160 --> 00:19:11,880
It lies at the end of
the Northern Silk Road —

173
00:19:11,880 --> 00:19:15,480
a branch of a huge trade
network being set up by China

174
00:19:15,560 --> 00:19:17,680
to connect with
the rest of the world.

175
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:27,320
In Duisburg, money is being
pumped into the debt-laden budget

176
00:19:27,560 --> 00:19:29,720
by cooperation with China.

177
00:19:30,840 --> 00:19:35,400
But Chinese collaboration isn’t always
this beneficial for its partners.

178
00:19:42,960 --> 00:19:45,160
This map shows hard-up countries

179
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:47,400
that built infrastructure
for the Silk Road

180
00:19:47,400 --> 00:19:50,640
by taking out huge
loans from Chinese banks.

181
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:03,640
China expert Thomas Heberer is aware

182
00:20:03,640 --> 00:20:06,840
that China’s loans can
also have a positive effect.

183
00:20:41,040 --> 00:20:44,240
This is how economic
growth is created in Africa.

184
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:48,000
However, China grants
loans without assessing

185
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:51,080
whether the recipients can
manage the money properly.

186
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:53,800
Heberer finds this problematic.

187
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:31,760
In the US, where people think big,
debts are breaking all records.

188
00:21:33,760 --> 00:21:38,520
During the Covid pandemic, the
country’s obligations skyrocketed.

189
00:21:43,880 --> 00:21:47,800
The debt clock currently stands
at over 33 trillion dollars.

190
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:07,840
This amount is still rising and has
serious consequences for society,

191
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,200
says banker Richard Vague.

192
00:22:11,400 --> 00:22:14,280
Growth in debt increases inequality.

193
00:22:15,560 --> 00:22:21,080
The reason is
that the top 10% of most economy,

194
00:22:21,080 --> 00:22:22,760
certainly in the United States,

195
00:22:23,200 --> 00:22:30,600
own 60 to 70% of the real estate
and stock in that economy.

196
00:22:31,360 --> 00:22:35,800
Increase debt levels,
increase the value of real estate,

197
00:22:36,080 --> 00:22:37,960
and increase the value of stocks.

198
00:22:42,760 --> 00:22:46,800
Financial expert Sandra Navidi
understands the impact

199
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:50,320
of unequal wealth distribution
on US society.

200
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:42,800
The economically weakest
in society suffer the most.

201
00:23:43,720 --> 00:23:47,720
Mississippi has the highest
proportion of poverty in the USA.

202
00:23:48,360 --> 00:23:52,040
Known debtors make
up 29% of the population,

203
00:23:52,840 --> 00:23:56,800
7 percentage points higher
than the national average.

204
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:04,200
For Annita Husband, severe debt
led to drastic repercussions.

205
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:09,200
Years ago, me and my husband, we were
going through financial difficulty.

206
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:11,960
He wasn't working.
I was working.

207
00:24:12,680 --> 00:24:15,560
I was in between pay cheques,

208
00:24:15,560 --> 00:24:19,560
because I started a new job
as an office manager.

209
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:23,920
One morning I came in to work,
my truck was being repossessed.

210
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:27,720
Another coworker came in and she
said 'I'm gonna tell you what to do'.

211
00:24:28,160 --> 00:24:33,200
She said:  'Write a check
for John Doe. For 365.

212
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:39,480
Get the cash, then when
you get paid, replace the cash'.

213
00:24:40,040 --> 00:24:44,480
Okay, so I did that.
And, um, payday comes

214
00:24:44,480 --> 00:24:47,560
and, of course, you know,
I have a million other bills,

215
00:24:47,560 --> 00:24:49,400
so I didn't replace the cash back.

216
00:24:49,400 --> 00:24:51,000
So, you know,
I'm thinking in my head,

217
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:55,680
okay, this is pretty easy!
So, I started, um...

218
00:24:55,880 --> 00:24:57,600
I guess forming a habit.

219
00:24:57,680 --> 00:25:01,680
I would keep my pay cheque
and I would pay my light bill with

220
00:25:02,160 --> 00:25:05,200
the fake cheques or
the fraudulent cheques.

221
00:25:07,480 --> 00:25:11,520
Altogether, Annita
embezzled $13,000.

222
00:25:11,680 --> 00:25:13,400
Her larceny was found out.

223
00:25:13,640 --> 00:25:16,240
She was sentenced
to five years’ probation

224
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:18,240
and had to pay back the money.

225
00:25:21,400 --> 00:25:23,920
She was in a mess
because she lost her job.

226
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:27,680
Her husband suffered a stroke
and was confined to bed.

227
00:25:31,960 --> 00:25:35,320
Annita regularly reported
to a probation officer.

228
00:25:35,520 --> 00:25:39,080
In return for his services,
he expected fees.

229
00:25:40,920 --> 00:25:43,960
They have what they call
a supervision fee.

230
00:25:43,960 --> 00:25:47,600
We have to pay 60 dollars a month.
I didn't have it.

231
00:25:47,760 --> 00:25:52,800
I didn't even pay on the
13,000 dollar restitution.

232
00:25:52,920 --> 00:25:57,320
You know, no one's working.
So the probation officer said:

233
00:25:57,320 --> 00:26:01,080
'Well, if you don't have it, that
is a violation of your probation,

234
00:26:01,080 --> 00:26:03,800
because you have
to report and pay.'

235
00:26:04,080 --> 00:26:06,640
So, I'm not going to leave

236
00:26:06,640 --> 00:26:12,360
my basically paralyzed husband,
three little kids home,

237
00:26:12,440 --> 00:26:14,240
so I'm not gonna come back.

238
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:22,640
Annita was arrested and
put in a restitution center.

239
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:26,160
This means a debt prison
in Jackson, Mississippi,

240
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:30,120
where prisoners serve time until
they have worked off their debts.

241
00:26:33,720 --> 00:26:37,280
These debts might be
damages owed to victims of crime

242
00:26:37,560 --> 00:26:39,680
or overdue court costs.

243
00:26:40,720 --> 00:26:44,680
Even for trifling amounts
below $1,000,

244
00:26:44,680 --> 00:26:47,560
people can end up in
this converted motel.

245
00:26:48,120 --> 00:26:51,760
But it has nothing to do
with holiday accommodation.

246
00:26:53,720 --> 00:26:56,360
It's barbed-wire,
just like a jail.

247
00:26:56,840 --> 00:27:01,240
When you get in, they go through your
stuff, just like a jail, a prison.

248
00:27:01,800 --> 00:27:04,520
They strip search you,
just like a prison.

249
00:27:04,600 --> 00:27:07,360
And I'll never forget this:
They brought me there on a Friday.

250
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:13,080
Mother's Day was on a Sunday.
So. They allow me one call.

251
00:27:13,400 --> 00:27:15,720
I called my son, my oldest son,

252
00:27:15,960 --> 00:27:19,360
and the first thing he said was
'Happy Mother's Day, mother!'

253
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:23,360
And that was so heart-breaking,
because I'm not gonna be there.

254
00:27:34,240 --> 00:27:37,800
Besides the prison-like
conditions in the restitution center,

255
00:27:37,800 --> 00:27:39,920
Annita was in for another shock.

256
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:43,960
She was charged 11 dollars
a day for board and lodging,

257
00:27:44,040 --> 00:27:46,000
which was added to her debts.

258
00:27:49,440 --> 00:27:50,680
Inmates even had to pay

259
00:27:50,680 --> 00:27:53,440
when the center was seeking
employment for them,

260
00:27:53,520 --> 00:27:55,480
no matter how long it took.

261
00:28:00,320 --> 00:28:02,920
Annita was relieved
to get a part-time job

262
00:28:02,920 --> 00:28:06,080
at Church’s Chicken,
a fast-food restaurant.

263
00:28:08,360 --> 00:28:12,640
Annita remembers that she had
high hopes of escaping the system.

264
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:17,200
By doing additional tasks,
she earned valuable bonus-points

265
00:28:17,200 --> 00:28:20,000
that could be used to
reduce her time in custody.

266
00:28:20,840 --> 00:28:23,040
But she had a rude awakening.

267
00:28:23,720 --> 00:28:26,000
I was, you know, scrubbing vans.

268
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:30,040
I was working in the cafeteria,
I was doing everything.

269
00:28:30,320 --> 00:28:35,720
Got my merits, so I went to the Warden
and she read my sentencing paper.

270
00:28:36,560 --> 00:28:39,720
Now, here I'm thinking all I had to
do was get, you know, so many merits

271
00:28:39,720 --> 00:28:42,040
and I can go back
to my normal life.

272
00:28:43,080 --> 00:28:48,120
She said: 'No, you have to stay here
until 13,000 dollars is paid off'.

273
00:28:56,400 --> 00:29:02,120
With a part-time hourly wage
of $7.25, less detention costs,

274
00:29:02,400 --> 00:29:05,240
Annita’s release was
a distant prospect.

275
00:29:09,120 --> 00:29:11,240
It became apparent
that she’d have to

276
00:29:11,240 --> 00:29:14,600
serve the maximum term
of five years in Jackson.

277
00:29:21,640 --> 00:29:27,520
Mississippi is joined by states
like Iran, Egypt and Afghanistan

278
00:29:27,520 --> 00:29:29,640
that also imprison debtors.

279
00:29:32,280 --> 00:29:35,880
Nowhere else does being
in debt lead to incarceration.

280
00:29:50,840 --> 00:29:54,920
We’re interested in finding out
what sort of people employ debtors,

281
00:29:54,920 --> 00:29:57,680
like Annita from
the restitution center.

282
00:29:59,760 --> 00:30:04,160
Businesswoman K.K. Kent
has several decades’ experience

283
00:30:04,240 --> 00:30:06,200
of such temporary workers.

284
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:11,600
When I was 19, I bought
my first rental properties

285
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:15,120
and a lot of them had to
be excavated, remodeled.

286
00:30:15,360 --> 00:30:20,080
And it was hard to find labor,
especially labor that was sober.

287
00:30:20,720 --> 00:30:25,520
So the restitution center opened up
here in Greenwood when I was 19.

288
00:30:26,120 --> 00:30:31,520
And I used to go and get labor to help
me do all my day-to-day operations:

289
00:30:31,520 --> 00:30:35,360
Handle my livestock, cows, horses,

290
00:30:35,360 --> 00:30:38,520
load hay, unload hay,
load feed, unload feed.

291
00:30:39,040 --> 00:30:42,240
Just stuff you have to do
every day when you're on a farm.

292
00:30:44,680 --> 00:30:49,560
But the staff she recruits from the
restitution center cause her problems.

293
00:30:51,680 --> 00:30:53,560
I may go through four or five

294
00:30:53,560 --> 00:30:56,200
before I find one that I'm
compatible with and I can work with.

295
00:30:57,280 --> 00:30:59,800
Because a lot of them
think they're entitled.

296
00:31:00,560 --> 00:31:03,280
And as soon as you pick
them up, they start asking,

297
00:31:03,520 --> 00:31:04,800
Can I use your phone?

298
00:31:05,120 --> 00:31:07,280
Will you stop at the store
and buy me a drink?

299
00:31:07,520 --> 00:31:09,200
Will you buy me some cigarettes?

300
00:31:09,640 --> 00:31:11,440
And there's been some
that I've picked up

301
00:31:11,440 --> 00:31:13,280
and hadn't even driven a half a mile,

302
00:31:14,040 --> 00:31:17,920
that just, their demeanor has —

303
00:31:18,560 --> 00:31:21,440
I didn't approve of it and I turned
around and took them right back

304
00:31:22,080 --> 00:31:24,200
and sent them back in
and got two more.

305
00:31:27,800 --> 00:31:30,160
Some are very appreciative.
Some are thankful.

306
00:31:30,240 --> 00:31:32,520
Grateful for everything
you do for them.

307
00:31:33,080 --> 00:31:35,520
You know, I've had some of them say,

308
00:31:35,520 --> 00:31:38,040
you know, if I had a
mom like you growing up,

309
00:31:38,360 --> 00:31:40,800
you know, my life would
have been totally different.

310
00:31:41,320 --> 00:31:42,680
I don't try to cure them.

311
00:31:42,680 --> 00:31:45,520
I just tell them, you know,
you need to pray about yourself.

312
00:31:46,280 --> 00:31:48,280
You need to figure out
your own self-discipline

313
00:31:48,280 --> 00:31:49,520
and do something about yourself.

314
00:31:49,520 --> 00:31:51,760
You don't need to look
for other people,

315
00:31:51,760 --> 00:31:54,360
because it's not other people's
problem, it’s your problem.

316
00:32:00,840 --> 00:32:04,960
Annita Husband was in over
her head with her problems.

317
00:32:07,880 --> 00:32:09,480
She had to think of something.

318
00:32:09,480 --> 00:32:12,360
Otherwise, given the
amount of money she owed,

319
00:32:12,400 --> 00:32:15,120
it would be years
before she was released.

320
00:32:17,440 --> 00:32:19,080
13,000 dollars!

321
00:32:19,240 --> 00:32:21,560
With a minimum
part-time paying job.

322
00:32:22,360 --> 00:32:26,240
I said: Annita, you need a plan.
You know, you need a plan.

323
00:32:27,920 --> 00:32:29,720
I just took my chances and ran.

324
00:32:31,560 --> 00:32:33,640
But the challenges were huge.

325
00:32:34,120 --> 00:32:36,640
She was only allowed
to leave to go to work.

326
00:32:37,240 --> 00:32:39,840
She was strictly
searched twice a day.

327
00:32:40,320 --> 00:32:43,880
And she only got $10
a week for personal use.

328
00:32:48,040 --> 00:32:51,240
They used to give us a roll of
quarters, you couldn't have dollars.

329
00:32:51,960 --> 00:32:55,840
So, every morning I would
wear my hair in a pony tail.

330
00:32:56,040 --> 00:33:01,440
I would put the roll
of quarters in my hair,

331
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:03,760
cause you know, in
between the pony tail.

332
00:33:03,760 --> 00:33:05,000
I would leave with
a roll of quarters.

333
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:08,200
Come back, I would change
the quarters for dollars.

334
00:33:08,400 --> 00:33:12,320
I would roll the dollars or
two fives, put it in my hair,

335
00:33:12,320 --> 00:33:14,000
cause I can get change at work.

336
00:33:16,200 --> 00:33:18,240
I hand-washed my clothes, you know,

337
00:33:18,240 --> 00:33:21,320
stuff that I would
take the ten dollars for,

338
00:33:21,680 --> 00:33:23,680
I would, you know, do without.

339
00:33:24,120 --> 00:33:26,160
So I saved up
enough for a bus ticket.

340
00:33:26,440 --> 00:33:31,520
This one particular day I planned to
leave, I had on my Church's uniform,

341
00:33:31,520 --> 00:33:35,080
but had on a pair of
blue jeans and a shirt

342
00:33:35,840 --> 00:33:38,040
under my Church's Chicken uniform.

343
00:33:38,080 --> 00:33:41,800
So I got a friend to take
me to the bus station

344
00:33:43,400 --> 00:33:46,160
and I got on the bus
and I came to Biloxi.

345
00:33:51,240 --> 00:33:54,000
Eventually, Annita
was arrested there

346
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:58,720
and had to serve part of her suspended
sentence in an ordinary prison.

347
00:34:00,200 --> 00:34:02,520
After nine months,
she was released.

348
00:34:02,920 --> 00:34:04,960
Her debts were cancelled.

349
00:34:10,840 --> 00:34:12,800
She doesn’t have a good word to say

350
00:34:12,800 --> 00:34:15,240
about the system
of restitution centers.

351
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:20,880
My debt was pretty large, but
there has been people that spent,

352
00:34:21,560 --> 00:34:24,800
at the restitution center
with maybe a 2,000 dollar debt,

353
00:34:24,960 --> 00:34:27,800
and three years later
they're still in the restitution.

354
00:34:28,240 --> 00:34:31,800
You say, well, one day I'm gonna
get to go home and, you know,

355
00:34:31,800 --> 00:34:34,040
be with my family,
but you don't know when.

356
00:34:36,880 --> 00:34:41,000
I felt like restitution centers
should be wiped out.

357
00:34:41,520 --> 00:34:44,880
It is just an undercover of slavery.

358
00:35:05,400 --> 00:35:07,920
The systemic discrimination
of Black people

359
00:35:07,920 --> 00:35:11,200
after slavery
continues to this day.

360
00:35:12,480 --> 00:35:16,240
It’s not just the neglected
cemeteries of the freed slaves

361
00:35:16,360 --> 00:35:20,280
— like this one in New Jersey —
that bear witness to this.

362
00:35:21,160 --> 00:35:24,640
More than a quarter of Blacks
can’t get loans at all,

363
00:35:24,680 --> 00:35:27,520
compared to just 17% of whites.

364
00:35:28,080 --> 00:35:29,560
And in Black neighborhoods,

365
00:35:29,560 --> 00:35:33,360
debt collectors pursue debtors
especially often and hard.

366
00:35:33,760 --> 00:35:36,880
Court cases are more
frequent than for whites.

367
00:35:40,680 --> 00:35:46,640
Differences in the USA concern not
only skin color, but also generations.

368
00:35:47,280 --> 00:35:49,600
In the majority-white middle class,

369
00:35:49,600 --> 00:35:52,800
twenty-somethings are groaning
under tuition fees

370
00:35:52,800 --> 00:35:54,560
which are higher than ever.

371
00:35:58,320 --> 00:36:01,880
The cost of student loans
in the US is rising sharply.

372
00:36:03,640 --> 00:36:09,560
In 2006, American students owed half a
trillion dollars in student loan debt.

373
00:36:09,760 --> 00:36:13,560
This figure has since
risen to $1.7 trillion.

374
00:37:18,400 --> 00:37:22,520
If the government
taxes individuals,

375
00:37:23,640 --> 00:37:26,760
it means those individuals
are able to spend less.

376
00:37:27,480 --> 00:37:30,280
And a government
uses that to pay off debt,

377
00:37:30,480 --> 00:37:34,360
the government itself is not using
those tax dollars to spend more.

378
00:37:34,800 --> 00:37:36,800
So the math is obvious.

379
00:37:37,920 --> 00:37:41,840
The folks being taxed spend less.
The government spending the same.

380
00:37:42,080 --> 00:37:43,800
GDP shrinks.

381
00:37:44,680 --> 00:37:50,040
So tax where you repay debt
actually contracts GDP

382
00:37:50,160 --> 00:37:52,920
and creates the opposite
effect that you want.

383
00:37:55,360 --> 00:37:58,280
But what about starting
with government spending?

384
00:37:59,080 --> 00:38:01,800
Couldn’t states just save like mad

385
00:38:03,880 --> 00:38:06,560
until their debts
have disappeared?

386
00:38:34,120 --> 00:38:37,880
Much of what looks like a
solution can actually be harmful.

387
00:38:39,960 --> 00:38:43,800
If so, how can we get control
of the global debt problem?

388
00:38:49,280 --> 00:38:53,360
Perhaps there are old ideas
outside the world of finance?

389
00:38:55,720 --> 00:38:58,720
This leads us to a
synagogue in Brooklyn.

390
00:39:02,520 --> 00:39:06,280
Sandra Navidi meets
rabbi Emily Cohen.

391
00:39:09,440 --> 00:39:15,200
So I guess you could say that
debt isn't anything new in society.

392
00:39:17,920 --> 00:39:22,200
If we go back all the way
to some of our earliest texts,

393
00:39:23,880 --> 00:39:26,440
you find this discussion of debt.

394
00:39:26,440 --> 00:39:29,200
So, there's a concept called shmita.

395
00:39:29,520 --> 00:39:32,040
And what shmita is,
is a” land rest”.

396
00:39:32,040 --> 00:39:36,040
It's this idea that for
six years you plant grain,

397
00:39:36,040 --> 00:39:39,320
you plant your crops, you harvest
them as you normally would,

398
00:39:39,320 --> 00:39:41,320
and then in the seventh year,
you don't plant anything.

399
00:39:41,480 --> 00:39:45,000
It's a time where the land is
supposed to get a chance to reset.

400
00:39:45,200 --> 00:39:50,040
And as part of that process,
as part of that reset,

401
00:39:50,040 --> 00:39:52,040
there's also remission of debt.

402
00:39:52,320 --> 00:39:55,080
So there's this idea that
any debt that you have

403
00:39:55,080 --> 00:39:58,960
that you've taken on during the six
years leading up to the shmita year

404
00:39:58,960 --> 00:40:01,040
is released in the seventh year.

405
00:40:02,760 --> 00:40:07,440
It’s unlikely that this debt remission
was ever implemented to the letter.

406
00:40:09,640 --> 00:40:12,960
But the ideal of this,
the ideal of saying

407
00:40:12,960 --> 00:40:15,960
that people sometimes
will be in debt,

408
00:40:15,960 --> 00:40:19,440
that there will be people who
are in need, and that ultimately,

409
00:40:19,440 --> 00:40:21,200
that can't be something
that continues forever,

410
00:40:21,200 --> 00:40:24,080
I think that that ideal is
something to strive for,

411
00:40:24,080 --> 00:40:26,760
even if we haven't
figured out how yet.

412
00:40:28,280 --> 00:40:32,440
Critics claim that debt
remission might be an invitation

413
00:40:32,440 --> 00:40:34,400
to get recklessly into debt.

414
00:40:35,720 --> 00:40:38,480
But financial experts disagree.

415
00:40:39,160 --> 00:40:42,920
So the moral hazard argument is
the one that is always advanced.

416
00:40:43,280 --> 00:40:47,880
If we give an inch,
borrowers would take a mile.

417
00:40:48,440 --> 00:40:51,360
Irresponsible behavior
will proliferate.

418
00:40:52,960 --> 00:40:56,360
And yet when we examine the facts,
we see that that's not the case.

419
00:40:56,640 --> 00:41:00,960
Bankruptcy is a miserable experience
in the best of circumstances,

420
00:41:01,280 --> 00:41:03,600
for the average
individual borrower.

421
00:41:03,920 --> 00:41:06,120
It's not something
they go into lightly.

422
00:41:06,240 --> 00:41:10,360
And, in fact, when we
examine bankruptcy,

423
00:41:10,560 --> 00:41:15,120
it relates to just two or three
key things most of the time:

424
00:41:15,440 --> 00:41:22,160
unexpected health emergency,
unexpected job loss, and divorce.

425
00:41:22,600 --> 00:41:27,320
If those are the things that
constitute almost all bankruptcies,

426
00:41:27,560 --> 00:41:32,440
that's not evidence that there's
folks out there purposely (saying)

427
00:41:32,440 --> 00:41:36,560
‘Listen, I'll lose my job or
I'll have a health care emergency

428
00:41:36,560 --> 00:41:39,840
in order to get out of my debt.’
It just doesn't happen.

429
00:41:47,360 --> 00:41:51,600
Debt cuts for individuals are
often advisable out of compassion.

430
00:41:51,840 --> 00:41:54,880
But they also make sense
for the general public.

431
00:42:39,520 --> 00:42:42,560
We’re back in Duisburg,
the stronghold of debt.

432
00:42:45,040 --> 00:42:49,560
One of those who’s undergone
personal bankruptcy is Frank Wendler.

433
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:57,600
The pop singer and failed gas retailer
was overwhelmed by a mountain of debt.

434
00:42:59,080 --> 00:43:03,280
Eventually, he contacted
debt counsellor Adam Carfora,

435
00:43:03,280 --> 00:43:06,160
who helped him file
for personal bankruptcy.

436
00:45:01,200 --> 00:45:04,720
After three years, Frank Wendler
will be debt-free.

437
00:45:04,840 --> 00:45:08,440
He’s found a new rewarding job
as an ambulance driver.

438
00:45:09,240 --> 00:45:12,920
And in his private life, too, things
have taken a turn for the better.

439
00:46:24,440 --> 00:46:27,640
But even if all the debts in
the world were written off,

440
00:46:27,800 --> 00:46:29,800
how could the endless cycle

441
00:46:29,800 --> 00:46:34,040
of over-indebtedness snapping
shut every few years be stopped?

442
00:46:37,200 --> 00:46:41,800
Is it possible to imagine a world in
which much less debt is incurred?

443
00:46:56,120 --> 00:46:59,760
In the African neighborhood
in Paris known as Goutte D’Or,

444
00:46:59,880 --> 00:47:01,400
they’re looking for an answer.

445
00:47:07,120 --> 00:47:11,320
Aita Magassa, who runs an
estate agency for homes in Africa,

446
00:47:11,480 --> 00:47:13,800
believes in a traditional solution.

447
00:47:17,240 --> 00:47:20,680
Her clients, all French citizens
of African origin,

448
00:47:20,800 --> 00:47:23,160
had problems investing in Africa.

449
00:47:23,360 --> 00:47:25,640
The banks refused to get involved.

450
00:48:16,320 --> 00:48:19,800
“Tontines” are traditional
African savings schemes

451
00:48:19,800 --> 00:48:24,520
where women join together and
pay in a fixed amount at each meeting.

452
00:48:27,720 --> 00:48:30,400
Each time, the money collected
is distributed

453
00:48:30,400 --> 00:48:34,200
to one of the participants, who
can spend it as she chooses.

454
00:48:35,440 --> 00:48:38,480
Aita Magassa applied
the centuries-old system

455
00:48:38,480 --> 00:48:41,760
of tontines to property purchases.

456
00:49:10,120 --> 00:49:15,240
It states that Aita’s clients
must pay in ?250 every month.

457
00:49:16,120 --> 00:49:21,080
In turn, ?5,000 will be distributed
to each of the 20 group members

458
00:49:21,200 --> 00:49:24,040
to finance a house
or land in Africa.

459
00:49:27,480 --> 00:49:30,680
None of her clients has
to go into debt for a home.

460
00:49:30,800 --> 00:49:35,680
This is an unbeatable advantage
over the usual financing system.

461
00:49:39,840 --> 00:49:42,360
We learn that the
principle of tontines

462
00:49:42,360 --> 00:49:46,480
also works in Europe
at Fily Sidibe’s hairdressing salon.

463
00:49:47,400 --> 00:49:49,960
Aitia visits the resourceful lady,

464
00:49:50,080 --> 00:49:53,920
for whom a bank loan was never
an option to finance the shop.

465
00:50:16,480 --> 00:50:18,240
These two women show

466
00:50:18,240 --> 00:50:21,800
how our financial system can
become more people-friendly.

467
00:50:23,640 --> 00:50:27,040
But a world without any
debt at all might be illusory.

468
00:50:27,280 --> 00:50:30,000
Perhaps it’s not even desirable.

469
00:51:13,040 --> 00:51:15,880
Not everyone benefits
from our system,

470
00:51:16,200 --> 00:51:18,960
and debt has become
a trap for many.

471
00:51:21,160 --> 00:51:24,480
The world must act —
in everybody’s interest.

472
